// GGUF BF16 dequant kernel — the vision tower's weight type in Qwen3.8-27B Ridge
// (mmproj-Qwen3.8-27B-BF16.gguf: 110 tensors, 0.85 GiB).
// Derived from llama.cpp (MIT License, https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp): bf16 is the top 16
// bits of an f32 (ggml.c ggml_bf16_to_fp32_row). See THIRD-PARTY.md.
//
// BF16 is NOT a block quant: 2 bytes per element, no scale, no codebook, no packing — so this is
// the simplest kernel in the tree. `ld_bf16` is the language's own conversion (zero-extend the 16
// bits, shift into the top half of an f32, reinterpret), which is exactly ggml's definition, and
// the reference is checked against the independent `gguf` package before this is trusted.
// Thread = one output element (Float, 8 bytes).
bf16deq(q<Int>, o<Int>, n<Int>, z<Int>) -> {
    gid<Int> = blk_x() * dim_x() + tid_x()
    if (gid < n) {
        val<Float> = ld_bf16(q + gid * 2)
        # GGUF stores out-major; gemm needs [in,out] (VybForge#11). When z (= in-dim) > 0, write
        # transposed (see q4kdeq's comment for why).
        if (z > 0) {
            rr<Int> = gid / z
            cc<Int> = gid % z
            st_f64(o + cc * n / z * 8 + rr * 8, val)
        } else {
            st_f64(o + gid * 8, val)
        }
    }
}
